+\begin{remark}[Solver exponent and normalizer term in Theorem~\ref{thm:bb-envelope}(iii)]
+\label{rem:tb-count-envelope}
+Two glosses on Proposition~\ref{prop:tb-count-gates}(d), as used in Theorem~\ref{thm:bb-envelope}(iii). The $O(\ell^8)$ bound for the normalizer steps of the primes with $e_p > 1$ holds for every $\eps$; those steps are done over $\mathbb{Z}/p^{e_p}$ with Howell forms (Lemma~\ref{lem:tb-howell-rounds}; AI-checked, pending human review). The exponent $\hat\beta_c$ is the excess time exponent of the solvers over their arity, with $\log p$ folded in: every solver call at arity $M_d$ on at most $a$ equations runs within $M_d((a+1)\log_2 p)^{\hat\beta_c}\operatorname{polylog}(\ell/\eps)$. \cite{IvanyosSantha2015} states its solver's time only as polynomial; by Remark~\ref{rem:is-runtime}, granting its tape-layout sketch, every Schedule~1 call satisfies this with exponent $5$, since $T^{\mathrm{IS}}_d \le 64C_1c^3M_d((a+1)\log_2 p)^5$ (here $g_d \le a$, and $b = \lceil\log_2 p\rceil$ satisfies $g_d^2+b^2 \le 2((g_d+1)b)^2$ and $b \le 2\log_2 p$); the Schedule~2 calls are not bounded here, so $\hat\beta_c$ is not computed.
+\end{remark}
